R('b2-q20','a','memory,shuffle','Possible causes — Too few shuffle partitions; AQE configuration',
 'Spill can result when shuffle partitions are too large for task memory. Inspecting and tuning shuffle partitioning is a low-cost first step before assuming that more expensive hardware is required.',
 'Partition size affects memory per task; more total executors alone does not shrink one oversized partition.',
 'Reviewing spark.sql.shuffle.partitions, including supported auto partition selection, can reduce oversized tasks. This directly tests a documented spill/memory cause before increasing compute cost.',
 'Autoscaling adds workers but does not necessarily change the number or skew of shuffle partitions. It can add cost without fixing oversized per-task memory demand.',
 'Photon can accelerate supported operations, but changing engines is not the first evidence-based partition-size diagnosis. It does not automatically remove every spill source.',
 'Memory-optimized nodes may reduce spill but can raise cost. The scenario asks what to try first without significantly increasing spending, favoring partition investigation.')
R('b2-q21','d','liquid,predictive','Automatic liquid clustering; Convert partitioned tables',
 'Automatic liquid clustering adapts clustering keys to query patterns and works with predictive optimization on supported managed tables. Fixed partition/Z-order choices do not automatically follow changing filters.',
 'Partitioning and liquid clustering are alternative layouts, not cumulative settings.',
 'Manually clustering on the previous Z-order columns preserves a fixed key choice despite changing filters. It does not provide automatic adaptation to the workload.',
 'Caching can accelerate reused reads but does not redesign the table layout or choose appropriate clustering keys. It is not a substitute for fixing a mismatched data layout.',
 'Manually retuning Z-order can help a known stable workload, but the scenario emphasizes changing filters and automatic platform optimization. It retains ongoing manual maintenance.',
 'Migrating to automatic liquid clustering lets supported platform optimization choose keys based on workload patterns. Use a supported conversion path rather than attempting to keep the existing partition/Z-order scheme.')
R('b2-q22','b','liquid','When to use liquid clustering; Convert partitioned tables',
 'Clustering on customer_id and purchase_date aligns data skipping with the two filter dimensions. Existing partitioning must be replaced, not retained; current supported runtimes offer ALTER TABLE ... REPLACE PARTITIONED BY WITH CLUSTER BY.',
 'High-cardinality filtering favors clustering rather than creating many tiny partitions.',
 'Liquid clustering is not compatible with retaining conventional partitioning. The option explicitly keeps the incompatible layout, so it cannot implement the documented design.',
 'Customer and date clustering keys match the common filter predicates. Convert the partitioned table with the supported replacement syntax and runtime requirements before running optimization.',
 'Partitioning directly on a high-cardinality customer key can create many small partitions. It also misses the flexible multi-dimensional layout the scenario needs.',
 'Caching reuses previously read data but does not improve physical data skipping for varied customer/date filters. It does not solve the stated layout issue.')
R('b2-q23','c','pipeline-modes,pipeline-config','Continuous pipeline mode; Compute configuration',
 'Continuous pipelines keep processing source changes until stopped, independent of whether each dataset is materialized or streaming. Production execution does not request retained idle development compute after stop.',
 'Triggered versus continuous determines update lifetime; table types do not make the entire pipeline one-shot.',
 'The continuous-update portion is plausible, but retaining compute for further testing is a development-oriented description. It does not match the production lifecycle assumption in the source.',
 'A one-update run does not match continuous processing. Keeping compute for testing also contradicts the production-oriented lifecycle stated.',
 'C describes continuous updates until the run is stopped and termination of its production compute lifecycle. Modern serverless orchestration manages resources automatically, but the source contrast is continuous rather than one-shot development behavior.',
 'Automatic shutdown after a single refresh describes a triggered pipeline. It conflicts with the explicitly configured continuous processing mode.')
