R('b2-q79','a','repair','Repair an unsuccessful job run',
 'Repair run can rerun failed Task 3 and the required downstream portion while retaining successful Tasks 1 and 2. The multi-task condition is satisfied in this four-task job.',
 'Repair is for a recorded failed run; Run now starts a new run.',
 'Repair run selects the failed work and dependents in the existing run’s recovery workflow. It avoids repeating successful earlier tasks unnecessarily.',
 'Reset job is not the run recovery action documented for this purpose. Resetting resource configuration is different from selecting failed task executions.',
 'Cloning a task changes the job definition and does not resume the failed execution. It can create duplicate work rather than repairing the recorded run.',
 'Run now starts a fresh execution of the job. It does not inherently skip the already successful first two tasks of the failed run.')
R('b2-q80','c','serverless-jobs,compute','Run a job with serverless compute',
 'Serverless jobs compute manages provisioning and scaling for supported scheduled tasks and avoids holding an interactive cluster idle. This minimizes manual cluster operations, though runtime, network, and library compatibility still matter.',
 'Predictable schedules do not require manually managed clusters when the workload supports serverless jobs.',
 'SQL warehouses are for supported SQL analytics tasks, not an automatic replacement for every general ETL job. The task language and execution interface still matter.',
 'Classic all-purpose compute can remain idle between overnight jobs and requires cluster lifecycle management. It is not the lowest operational overhead option here.',
 'Serverless jobs compute directly targets managed provisioning and job execution without manually maintaining a cluster. It best matches the stated overhead and idle-cost goals among the choices.',
 'Classic jobs compute also avoids much idle cost, but the user still manages cluster configurations and sizing choices. Serverless more directly meets the minimal operational overhead requirement.')
R('b2-q81','a','job-table-trigger','Trigger jobs when tables are updated',
 'A table update trigger starts a job in response to updates to monitored Unity Catalog tables, rather than merely checking cloud object arrivals or a wall-clock schedule. Processing still needs its own incremental state.',
 'A changed table is a different event source from a newly arrived object-store file.',
 'The table update trigger is specifically designed for an upstream table-change dependency. Configure the monitored table and desired trigger conditions in the job.',
 'A file-arrival trigger watches storage paths, not the committed update state of a Unity Catalog table. It is a different event boundary.',
 'A scheduled trigger runs according to time, whether or not the Bronze table changed. It does not directly express reacting to the table update.',
 'A continuous trigger keeps the job running/restarting according to its continuous lifecycle. It is not a per-table-change trigger.')
